integ5: glm_mm_reduce_two (glue5's two-problem reduce) takes pdl5's reduce rule (TF_GLM_PDL_CHAIN's reduce).

- glm_mm.cu: glm_mm_reduce_two calls qmm_frag::grid_wait() right after its existing qmm_frag::grid_launch(), before it
  reads `part`, writes `out` or exits, exactly as pdl5 0067 made glm_mm_reduce do.
- cuda_mm.matmulTwo: the reduce launches with `.pdl = k.chain.reduce`, as matmul's reduce does since pdl5 0067 (the
  two-problem matmul itself already launched with `.pdl = k.pdl`, as glm_mm). The shared expert's side stream never
  calls matmulTwo (DSA prepare runs on the main stream).
- glm_mm_policy_host.zig: the reduce's attribute follows chain.reduce at 1/8/32 rows (glue5's own test keeps the chain
  off and still sees an ordinary launch).
Without this the stack is correct too (an ordinary launch waits for the matmul), but the DSA layers' two-problem
reduces would lose pdl5's reduce overlap (up to 2 a DSA layer, q_b's pair only with sparse rows: 11-22 a step). Exact by construction: the same arithmetic;
every access to the matmul's partials after the wait. TF_GLM_PDL_CHAIN without `reduce`, or TF_GLM_PDL=0, launches
it plainly; TF_GLM_MM_TWO=0 takes the two lone matmul + reduce launches instead.

